		{
			"id": "e-zYeK7SYimNNdqok",
			"description": "Ethereal Honour Blade",
			"local_notes": "Mundane: no power field and no armour divisor. Parry +2 as the Basic Set Quarterstaff (the blade sits on a long two-handed haft). At an Ethereal's ST 9 (sw 1d-1, thr 1d-2) it swings 1d+1 cut and thrusts 1d imp. It is a badge of rank first and a weapon last.\n\nLore: Codex: T'au Empire background shows Ethereals carrying honour blades as marks of their caste and authority, and treats them as ceremonial weapons that an Ethereal will nonetheless wield if he must; the Ethereal caste's real power is that the T'au obey them, not that they can fight. I hold the two-handed staff-like form from the framework's brief and do not have a source for its exact length or weight.\n\nDesign: Framework: 'two-handed staff/polearm style, mundane'. Built as a Basic Set quarterstaff-length haft with a blade (glaive family): swing sw+2 cut, thrust thr+2 imp, reach 1,2, parry +2 (framework: quarterstaff-type weapons parry +2), skill Staff. Exact dice at ST 9: the swing is 1d+1 (average 4.5, cut x1.5 wounding): penetrates flak (DR 30) 0.0% of hits, carapace 0.0%, Astartes plate 0.0%. Against an unarmoured person it does about 7 injury, enough to wound a T'au or human but never to cut through armour. That is deliberate: an Ethereal is not built to duel, and the framework's Ethereal (ST 9, DX 11) is a leader, not a fighter. Handling: 4 lb, Min ST 8, two-handed (dagger). TL 8: a mundane blade (framework: mundane, so no TL 11 superscience), cost $3,000 for a ceremonial object of fine make.",
			"tags": [
